  <h1>Sova: the EVM that reads Zcash</h1>
  <p class="dim">sova.io/testnet · public testnet live</p>

  <div class="post">
    <p class="lead">Zcash is private money that works. Sova lets programs react to it.</p>

    <p>Sova is an EVM sidechain that runs beside Zcash. Every Sova node runs a full EVM beside its own Zcash node, so a contract can check a ZEC payment against Zcash itself, and the ZEC stays where it was paid. Its gas, SOVA, is issued only by burning ZEC. One Sova block per Zcash block.</p>

    <p>Every SOVA begins as ZEC burned for good. Sova’s footprint on Zcash is the burns, at Zcash’s normal fee.</p>

    <p>A public testnet has been running since 24 September, mined with testnet ZEC. Start with the <a href={repo.blob(TESTNET.guide)}>join guide</a>.</p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p><strong>Mining is burning.</strong> Send ZEC to a script nobody can spend from, in an ordinary Zcash transaction that names your EVM address. The matching Sova block mints SOVA to you. Everyone who burned in that Zcash block splits the reward by weight, and the top burner seals the block. Any amount from 1,000 zatoshis.</p>
    <p><strong>Every node checks every mint.</strong> Each node re-derives the mints from its own Zcash node and rejects any block that disagrees, at the tip and in synced history.</p>
    <p><strong>Contracts read Zcash.</strong> A contract can check whether a ZEC payment landed, to which address and how deep (SIP-4), and the value in each shielded pool, block by block (SIP-7). Both are live on the testnet. Contracts are public. They read Zcash’s shielded economy in aggregate, never inside it.</p>

    <h2>Fair launch</h2>
    <p>No premine. No allocation to investors, the team or anyone. Nothing in escrow.</p>
    <p>SOVA comes into existence only when someone burns ZEC and seals a block, and whoever does earns it.</p>

    <h2>Where it came from</h2>
    <p>Sova began as a venture-backed project to bring Bitcoin into an EVM. We decided not to go ahead with that plan, and set it aside. The company turned to other work, mostly intent-based solving, and much of that was Zcash on NEAR.</p>
    <p>Most sidechains for Bitcoin have been built by companies and depend on them. Zcash leaves room to try the other way: a chain that belongs to no company, launched fairly and run by the people who use it. Sova came back in that form, as a public-good experiment in support of the Zcash network. Sova Labs, the company, open-sourced the project as Sova Chain. Think of ConsenSys and Ethereum: Sova Labs builds for the chain and doesn’t own it. We run some of the nodes and mine, as anyone can. <a href="/story">The full story</a>.</p>

    <h2>Join the testnet</h2>
    <ul class="bul">
      <li><strong>The <a href={repo.blob(TESTNET.guide)}>join guide</a></strong> goes from nothing to a synced Zcash node, a Sova node that checks the genesis hash, a funded miner, and sealing blocks with your own key if you want to.</li>
      <li><strong>Release v0.1.20</strong> ships prebuilt binaries for Linux x86_64 and Apple Silicon.</li>
      <li><strong>A zebrad snapshot</strong> shortens the Zcash sync from hours to minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>The faucet</strong> at <a href={`${TESTNET.faucet}/status`}><code>faucet-testnet.sova.io</code></a> sends 0.1 testnet ZEC a day per address, enough for a few hundred burns.</li>
      <li><strong>The explorer</strong> at <a href={TESTNET.explorer}><code>explorer.testnet.sova.io</code></a> shows blocks and transactions.</li>
      <li><strong>Chain ID {TESTNET.chainId}.</strong> Public RPC at <code>rpc-testnet.sova.io</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>To try it with no network at all, <code>./box/up.sh</code> in a clone of the repo runs a Zcash regtest node, a Sova node and a miner on your laptop.</p>

    <h2>Ashwings</h2>
    <p><a href="/ashwings">Ashwings</a> are 10,000 pixel owls drawn by their own contract on Sova. The testnet mint is open: 625 SOVA, or 0.05 testnet ZEC from any testnet Zcash wallet, with the contract checking the payment on Zcash itself. Testnet owls go away when the testnet resets.</p>

    <h2>An experiment</h2>
    <p>Sova is pre-release and unaudited. Mainnet is expected in Q1 2027; dates are targets. The code and the specs still change, and testnet state gets reset. Burned ZEC is gone for good. Don’t put in money you can’t afford to lose.</p>

